me walk around  the space in person and say this is what  I want the regions to be physically say  this space that I'm standing right now  is going to be one corner of this region  which as an aside I am very very  fortunate if any of you have dealt with  GPS on smartphones before you know that  it can be very very inaccurate  especially when you are in large cities  like San Francisco I don't have time in  this talk I have given talks about this  and how GPS works under the hood so this  was in San Francisco which being in a  big city means that there are a lot of  tips and tricks to improve location  services but it is also not near any  buildings not near any Hills and right  on the waterfront which meant in  practice I was getting five meters or  less of GPS accuracy which is completely  unheard of but it's also magical because  it meant I could stay in artists brain  and work on thinking about location as  just this is the Platonic ideal of where  the player is rather than having to  [ __ ] around with data being imperfect  and having to design around that so the  other the other thing that this drove  out was I knew early on that I cared a  lot about the transitions between spaces  and for a lot of the different reasons I  ended up just smoothly fading between  the two for any given line of poetry if  you're at an overlap I will generate you  one or the other but I knew that even  though it was important to the piece  that you can do this you can start  anywhere you can go anywhere you can do  it for five minutes or five hours a lot  of people were going to start by the  fort because that's where a lot of the  parking is and in the fort the first  place they are probably going to go that  is a meaningful shift like there are a  couple different reasons in the fort but  broadly speaking there's a set of stairs  that lead up to the park and this was  going to be a lot of people's first  experience with huh is this piece  actually going to respond to me what  does it feel like for this to be  generative  so this space felt really important to  sort out how did this actually change  and so I started out using the iPhones  altimeter if you have a newer ish iPhone  it has a very very precise sensor that  can tell you exactly how high above sea  level you are to the level that on these  stairs I could know exactly which step  you were on but that turned out again to  be completely illegible especially when  the granularity of the rest of the piece  is like oh yeah you're sort of in a  grassy area or you're sort of by the  cannons saying you're on the bottom  third of the step versus the middle  third did not make any sense it didn't  even seem legible to separate it out  based on are you coming down the stairs  or are you going up the stairs so the  end result I don't know which of these  buttons is a laser pointer well yeah you  can sort of see there is a dedicated  region that is just the stairs so while  you are walking on the stairs you hear  poetry that vaguely references sort of  elevating yourself but that's about it  and all of this was only possible  because I was able to do all of this on  site in the physical space which is  really difficult given that I don't live  in San Francisco so I assume most of the  people in this room are software people  of some flavor and we are used to the  idea that we have very very tight  feedback loops and there are there like  gradations in there like writing an  executing unit test is generally faster  than higher-level automation tests is  faster than manual QA is faster than  deploying something to end-users and  waiting for data to flow in but that is  also still a relatively bounded time  space as opposed to hardware or real  world stuff is really really hard  especially when in my case where that  might mean all right I'm gonna be in San  Francisco in two weeks and I might have  a day or two what can I get done so I  needed to be able to really optimize  that time in the physical space which  meant being able to do everything that  didn't need being in the physical space  and do that anywhere so I partition this  out into a couple different groups of  things where they I think this  of high-level tactical thing here is it  is worth taking time to design your  design process so yeah I had three  different bundles of work the first was  I had this poetry generator it needs to  be able to spit out poetry I need to  figure out what are the range of  temperatures that are valid I need to  test given some hypotheses about here's  some seed text that will generate poetry  that is both interesting and relevant to  the broad sort of space I can do that on  my computer without anything else once I  had that here hearing like reading  poetry in my computer or having a  text-to-speech BOTS read poetry back at  me still doesn't give me the aesthetic  experience of walking through a physical  space which I can still capture without  having to be at the specific Park so I  had a special build of the app that  wasn't region-locked to Fort Mason I  could just open the app anywhere and it  would read out poetry and I could press  a button to change regions so I could  still get a sense for here's what this  type of poetry feels like as I am  walking through the physical world as  I'm walking to like a different space  wherever I'm walking I can press a  button and simulate shifting regions in  the app and get an aesthetics feel for  how does this work at a high level in  the real world and then having all of  that taken care of  that and making sure that things were  such that I could always have my latest  code running on device that meant that  when I'm on site I could focus that on  on-site things  it's as I explained earlier that meant  high level conceptual questions like  what how should this work what does it  feel like to be doing this at all and  then later on this was essential for  tuning out fine-tune details like  sorting out have exactly those  transitions between spaces other than  the stairs worked and how to best  implement the subtle background audio so  I had these janet cardiff style  background sound scapes so while you're  in the community garden you hear birds  chirping when you're in the grassy park  area you hear kids playing when you're  by the chapel there are bells off in the  distance and my first attempts at those  were incredibly hand fisted and without  being able to sit there in the park and  try something out and then go and sit  down and at the Edit table and tweak  some variables and try it again  it it would have felt incredibly  overwrought and would have detracted  from the  rather than adding to it so I guess I'm  a high-level question though why bother  with any of this this is a lot of work  compared to just making an audio walk or  making a Twitter bot into a thing that I  care a lot about as an artist and I feel  like I talked about this way too much is  that the way you interact with any sort  of digital system meaningfully affects  your relationship to it even even  separate from arts like you feel  meaningfully different about an app you  use on your phone versus using the exact  same service as a web app on your  desktop like it is a different emotional  connection and even if you're using  using it on your computer with a MacBook  Pro flutterfly keyboard versus like a  juicy clack e mechanical keyboard and so  as an artists who is interested in  making things that connect people to  each other and other systems of the  world and get them excited about the  world why would you not play with that  artistic palette and use all of these  tools at your disposal so I think making  things like this and putting in this  effort is really important and hopefully  you do too but if not that's also okay  hopefully you have so glean something  useful out of this whether it is  thinking about emergence in immersive  theater or procedural text generation  with the neural networks or other  methods or thinking about designing  process to test integrations with  real-world data but above all hopefully  this is some sort of inspiration you  should go out and make some more weird  things in the world Thanks  [Applause]
 

